course: dictionary
lesson: 6592
title: Words about people in history
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A noblewoman is a woman who belongs to the nobility, the high ranks of society. Look at the parts of the word: noble and woman. A noblewoman has a title, such as duchess, countess or lady, which shows her high rank.
explain: Context gives you clues. «Lady Godiva was a real noblewoman of the 1000s.» The word «real» tells you she was a true person, and «Lady» is a title, so she must have belonged to the nobility.
explain: Do not guess from the sound of the word. A noblewoman is not simply a kind or good woman. Check your meaning by asking: does the sentence need a title or a high rank? If yes, you have the right idea.
fact: A noblewoman is a woman who belongs to the nobility.
fact: Titles for noblewomen include duchess, countess and lady.
fact: Noblewomen could run large households and estates.
fact: Some noblewomen, like Eleanor of Aquitaine, were very powerful.
fact: The plural of «noblewoman» is «noblewomen».
exercise: «Lady Godiva was a real noblewoman of the 1000s.» What is a noblewoman? | A woman who rules the sea | *A woman who belongs to the nobility, with a title like lady | A woman who makes bread for the king | A woman who lives in a small village
exercise: «Lady Godiva was a real noblewoman, but the story of her ride was first written down more than a hundred years after she died.» Find and copy one word that shows Lady Godiva was a real person. | story | *real | written | famous
exercise: Which word is the plural of «noblewoman»? | *noblewomen | noblewoman | nobleman | noble
test: Which title shows that a woman belongs to the nobility? | *Countess | Teacher | Baker | Farmer
test: «Some noblewomen were very powerful.» What does this tell you about noblewomen? | They always lived in small huts | *They could have great power and influence | They were always poor | They were never allowed to own land
exam: «Eleanor of Aquitaine was a powerful noblewoman who ran large estates.» What does noblewoman mean? | A child who is learning to read | A servant who cooks in the castle kitchen | A poor farm worker | *A woman of high rank who belongs to the nobility
